#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e985ada-2962-571b-bafe-6339b6b9c3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284468} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad8d16d-c707-512b-8f81-bf4dd6d4f4d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284469} {:answer/id #uuid "794a7a82-8c6b-5594-a73d-0aaee8817743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284470} {:answer/id #uuid "6da59769-1bbc-5fc8-9d77-d797da84d43b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284471} {:answer/id #uuid "0ae742dc-c2af-52d4-a36d-4a2bce1b501c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "794a7a82-8c6b-5594-a73d-0aaee8817743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284470} :answer-field/id #uuid "526f0052-909b-5399-aef6-079d28911781" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b69ae23-2c51-5627-87f4-4109cdd5f517" :question/math-academy-id "q-103440" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,4)$ and $(-4,6).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,4)$ and $(-4,6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{-4-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(2,3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{5}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{5}{2}x-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f311b5e-b5ac-5a5b-a26d-82348b2f89b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{63}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284475} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d878f1-16bf-5351-bfb2-207d5d2af168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284476} {:answer/id #uuid "dad0f8b1-1692-5294-be68-c5e70c71f41b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284477} {:answer/id #uuid "c5db9fa2-c9a4-53da-9218-cb0d279103b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{21}}{105}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284478} {:answer/id #uuid "be23f83f-e7bd-551f-ba45-ec485fa67973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{63}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dad0f8b1-1692-5294-be68-c5e70c71f41b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284477} :answer-field/id #uuid "14ff7fe9-ed2b-5456-9933-6bda9b422ee2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ed54f9b9-6bd4-505a-bea9-8bdf8d4751e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-24072" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/assets/b81b4602d221eb04aab76b0ed8853ab75a5c3cf93e76cc489344bc15133c4daf.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{5})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5})}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0040fc1-2134-5eaa-a3e8-6c7a457f75f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Data Set Using the Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f91ecf3c-df89-5000-b204-af48005278b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f91ecf3c-df89-5000-b204-af48005278b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284482} :answer-field/id #uuid "03b06a2f-17d0-50af-91dc-9bdb2bac7988" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "130f1ac3-a8e6-5057-ac85-84422bbb05be" :question/math-academy-id "q-263419" :question/problem "What is the variance of the following data set?\n\n$$\n3,\\,18,\\,16,\\,7,\\,1,\\,12,\\,5,\\,10\n$$\n\nDo not round your answer.\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=8$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (3+18+16+7+1+12+5+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 72 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot ({3}^{2}+{18}^{2}+{16}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{12}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{10}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (9+324+256+49+1+144+25+100) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 908 \\\\  & =\\frac{908}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{227}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{227}{2}-{(9)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{227}{2}-81 \\\\  & =\\frac{227-162}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{65}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d82fa3-d576-5c42-9dca-4e1b76473424" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8373b95a-daf0-5908-ace4-1a8391cd704e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8373b95a-daf0-5908-ace4-1a8391cd704e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284485} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5222668-bdf7-50a4-bb3a-15ec3086c2a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284484} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef7ad4c-cc05-5d57-814a-bd0411c3b2fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ef7ad4c-cc05-5d57-814a-bd0411c3b2fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284487} :answer-field/id #uuid "abd816cc-06af-5194-89fd-a97145570daf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284486} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83efee1e-f8cc-5e07-9af8-840d0a3ed158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83efee1e-f8cc-5e07-9af8-840d0a3ed158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284489} :answer-field/id #uuid "baf5396d-eafd-57c7-a859-6e88e15b7051" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3583bea-4e7e-51bc-8a8f-4cf29d2822e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-276683" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2-6x}-2\\,?$\n\nThe range is\n$f(x)\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\cup (${{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=-2.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/assets/27f800d93b5d4c84e98d0b7332388c5b3d513e4b896adabe6e410e4c3e3b684c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf98ee24-7774-5674-8a1e-63f4f6d225c0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f3f933-d6ea-5dde-99a2-93e85029be09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284492} {:answer/id #uuid "4505a44c-2f96-5e30-b699-0953a5168ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284493} {:answer/id #uuid "73270869-4f7d-5094-9fd2-24eb93103668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}|x|)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284494} {:answer/id #uuid "3855ff7a-93e8-5941-b04e-b8d0d9b456d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284495} {:answer/id #uuid "54b4881f-c44f-5353-a444-ec56219f7b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}|x|}{2}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3855ff7a-93e8-5941-b04e-b8d0d9b456d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284495} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb53b84a-13f4-57c1-bd29-5e534de53b22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74e5a7b3-a114-5a6d-a31d-10e14f2e45b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-29838" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "63080bb8-7a6a-505d-813a-e6c99d337064" :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting a Number and a Rational Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbcee5f1-3064-58fe-89c5-53a9c21df82d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4a - 11}{a - 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178584} {:answer/id #uuid "05c931cc-2a24-5621-9fa2-2013c4fce76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4a + 11}{a - 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178585} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbe4ec0-25ca-5324-bfb9-8cdefe9c4f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2a - 11}{a - 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178586} {:answer/id #uuid "7472fd81-6a9f-5263-913d-be7f3bca7264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2a - 7}{a - 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178587} {:answer/id #uuid "782d3871-d1c1-5acd-a7ce-87ad3a0fd18e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4a - 7}{a - 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cbe4ec0-25ca-5324-bfb9-8cdefe9c4f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2a - 11}{a - 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178586} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8507c22-d576-5fbf-8988-fb00a812daca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4906f3f-5058-5671-b3a5-090c5552dbcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-41566" :question/problem "Write $3-\\frac{a+2}{a-3}$ as a single fraction." :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $3=\\frac{3}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $a-3$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $a-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{a-3}{a-3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(a-3)}{a-3}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(a-3)}{a-3}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(a-3)-(a+2)}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3a-9-a-2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2a-11}{a-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15cd299b-c720-5bf6-814c-2bef2c839e48" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d61d591c-81f9-5a28-a530-079e24b9110a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x-4}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284499} {:answer/id #uuid "bdae5c28-3b3a-5d7a-8a83-c60d9ac5e4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11x-33}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284500} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4258db-3cb5-5afa-9c93-542ba0f9b20d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284501} {:answer/id #uuid "44fc373f-1776-54b0-8494-f28f3a88acb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{6}x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284502} {:answer/id #uuid "e20b120d-edfd-56fc-b0ca-d644d5e0ce0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{11x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdae5c28-3b3a-5d7a-8a83-c60d9ac5e4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11x-33}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284500} :answer-field/id #uuid "36d0d55f-1962-5c44-be87-c213635b0c7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "324405ab-b96b-5067-9341-26d9c7daf119" :question/math-academy-id "q-7024" :question/problem "If $f(x)=-\\frac{6}{11}x+3$, then ${f}^{-1}(x)=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{6}{11}x+3\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{6}{11}y+3\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{6}{11}y+3 \\\\ x-3 & =-\\frac{6}{11}y \\\\ 11(x-3) & =-6y \\\\ 11x-33 & =-6y \\\\ -\\frac{11x-33}{6} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\frac{11x-33}{6}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18cbcbac-d21b-5952-902d-84b4a72e0729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284506} {:answer/id #uuid "6371057a-b4b7-5cc9-add0-36af86fab5a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}{e}^{8{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284507} {:answer/id #uuid "6fcb2fbf-e228-5b4e-af79-33e1e9df18ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{x}^{3}{e}^{2{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284508} {:answer/id #uuid "3a05cf38-3a88-5b87-9b51-242e0ee2b3a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{x}^{3}{e}^{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284509} {:answer/id #uuid "312d0597-c5c4-5997-ae50-1e1ec7bf4db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}{e}^{2{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fcb2fbf-e228-5b4e-af79-33e1e9df18ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{x}^{3}{e}^{2{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284508} :answer-field/id #uuid "fed000e9-896d-5534-b223-5bab60a8d852" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60135420-a8bf-5933-9dc7-c9a4b55d70fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-71873" :question/problem "Given that $y={e}^{2{x}^{4}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (8{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}{e}^{2{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93eb24a9-f358-50cf-b447-c394864843f9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Combination of Functions Given the Necessary Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "267ec29c-ae1c-5066-b901-c4e2348206fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284513} {:answer/id #uuid "88b2109d-93fb-57c6-8296-b509b1ef1de1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284514} {:answer/id #uuid "90b7c3a8-cc25-5bf4-8d63-8588cd149dab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284515} {:answer/id #uuid "b20f92b5-9499-5297-9e19-d1be1ac2c43c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284516} {:answer/id #uuid "ae09de8b-cd41-51d4-b4f9-6d04e8c57be8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "267ec29c-ae1c-5066-b901-c4e2348206fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284513} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeab12df-247f-5228-9271-394c3a12f4a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be5c2481-ab3b-578e-a87d-89f009058881" :question/math-academy-id "q-72996" :question/problem "For the functions $e(x)$ and $n(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\ne(-5)=5,\\,{e}^{′}(-5)=-6,\\,n(-5)=-7,\\,{n}^{′}(-5)=9\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3e(x)+n(x))$ at $x=-5.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3e(x)+n(x))=3{e}^{′}(x)+{n}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-5$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3e(x)+n(x))|}_{x=-5} & =3{e}^{′}(-5)+{n}^{′}(-5) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-6)+9 \\\\  & =-18+9 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
